  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 AM EDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nThe inverted surface trough that helped spark some showers in our \nsouthern half on Monday will be pushed away to the NW as high \npressure over the Mid Atlantic moves southward to the Outer Banks. \nAloft, a 500 mb ridge will reestablish itself over the region today, \nleading to dry conditions across the area through Wednesday, with \nabove normal temperatures.\n\nThe midlevel ridge gets suppressed southward on Thursday as a broad, \npositively-tilted trough drops into the Great Lakes region. \nConvection along the associated cold front will move across KY \nThursday, possibly reaching our KY border counties by late \nafternoon. With the front remaining to our north, along with the \nmidlevel vort max and upper jet streak, convection will likely be \nweakening as it moves into our area in the evening along a pre-\nfrontal trough or outflow boundary. Instability is not particularly \nimpressive, so the potential for any strong to severe storms looks \nlow at this time. The nearly zonal midlevel flow parallels the \nsurface front, so it will likely stall to our north. The position of \nthe front with weak troughing to our west will keep some scattered \nto numerous in the area through Friday. \n\nThe weekend looks to start off with a weak trough over the MS River \npossibly bringing shower and storm activity Saturday. A building \nridge across the Southeast may bring a drier pattern next week, with \ntemperatures continuing above normal.",
